ity  (x15 / -))\n1973\t            :arg0  (x16 / 办法\n1974\t                  :mod  (x17 / 这))\n1975\t            :arg1  x11)\n1976\t      :aspect Performance\n1977\t      :modstr FullAff)\n1978\t```\n1979\t\n1980\tMulti-word concepts are also relevant for idiomatic expressions that are segmented into multiple words but mapped single concepts.\n1981\t\n1982\t<span id=\"3-1-3-7 (2b)\">3-1-3-7 (2b)</span> 我 把 锤子 、 螺钉 、 饥渴 、 死亡 ， 全都 抛在 脑 后 。\n1983\t```\n1984\t(x0 / 抛在脑后-01\n1985\t      :arg0  (x2 / individual-person\n1986\t                 :ref-person 1st\n1987\t\t\t :ref-number Singular)\n1988\t      :arg1  (x3 / and\n1989\t            :op1  (x4 / 锤子)\n1990\t            :op2  (x5 / 螺钉)\n1991\t            :op3  (x6 / 饥渴)\n1992\t            :op4  (x7 / 死亡))\n1993\t      :manner  (x11 / 全-01)\n1994\t      :manner  (x12 / 都)\n1995\t      :aspect Performance)\n1996\t```\n1997\t\n1998\t##### Part 3-1-3-8. Miscellaneous constructions\n1999\tOther than multi-word concepts, languages often have regular or irregular constructions that involve mapping multipe words to single concepts. We adopt the general approach of AMR, which is to propose a set of abstract predicates each of which has their semantic roles to annotate such constructions. <a href=\"#3-1-3-8 (1)\">(1)</a> is such an example, where the abstract concept ``correlate-91'' is proposed to annotate the ``the X-er, the Y-er`` construction. \n2000\t<!--which concepts are languages-specific, and which are uniform? -->\n2001\t\n2002\t\n2003\t<span id=\"3-1-3-8 (1)\">3-1-3-8 (1)</span> The more I read your stuff, the more I am convinced that you have a black heart.\n2004\t\n2005\t```\n2006\t (c2 / correlate-91\n2007\t       :aspect Habitual\n2008\t       :ARG1 (m / more\n2009\t                  :frequency-of (r / read-01"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"29fc94a9-7d58-4d89-83e2-d9515d08e518","uuid":"686ef192-2ccc-4a01-a3ef-9f5b37032542","timestamp":"2026-11-10T07:27:01.739Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":